\title{Discrete structures and induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{How much structure can a discrete model induce, and which choices must
already be supplied? A finite graph offers a small setting in which to ask
this question precisely. Its incidence data specify how vertex values differ
along edges; edge weights are further choices. The
\vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002} and
[alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) make these inputs explicit.}

\p{Choosing a discrete carrier, changing its coordinates, and eliminating
field variables are different operations. Reordering vertices changes a
matrix representation. Choosing new edge weights changes the weighted
model. To ask what eliminating some fields induces, we must also specify
an action and a measure, then compare expectations of the remaining
variables. A [positive quadratic action](fdip-0004) and
[Gaussian normalization](fdip-0005) give a probability law for the real
fields. [Polynomial observables](fdip-0006) then have finite expectations
in a positive normalized state. The [weighted graph differential and its adjoints](fdip-0007)
produce a [nonnegative edge energy](fdip-0008). On a cycle,
[finite differences obey a shifted product rule](fdip-0009).
The [massive Green operator](fdip-000A) is the
[coordinate covariance](fdip-000B) of this Gaussian law.
At zero mass, a [mean-zero constraint](fdip-000C) removes the constant
mode using a different field space and measure.
[Integrating out a block of fields](fdip-000D) induces a positive
effective precision and an explicit normalization factor. Its
[Gaussian marginal](fdip-000E) preserves every integrable retained
observable. [Eliminating alternate vertices](fdip-000F) computes both
objects on the four-cycle. A [finite Gaussian background](fdip-000G)
records the data to be compared. [Exact relabelings](fdip-000H) transport
the normalized state and a compatible retained marginal; a
[trace ratio](fdip-000I) separates two weighted cycles even after
positive scaling and relabeling of their Laplacians.}

\p{The [half-turn on vertex and edge fields](fdip-000J) gives a
nontrivial finite-group action. Its [Gaussian invariance](fdip-000K)
passes through the equations and the retained marginal; changing
weights or the retained partition shows which hypotheses are needed.}

\p{The [group-algebra action](fdip-000L) separates the same fields
into two half-turn sectors. Their [Gaussian blocks](fdip-000M)
and retained scalar precisions show what the symmetry organizes
while leaving weights and states free. In
[characteristic two](fdip-000N), the action survives but the
complementary-sector decomposition fails.}

\p{The [configuration quadratic form](fdip-000O) fixes the
polarization and sign conventions for its [Clifford algebra](fdip-000P).
[Form-preserving maps](fdip-000Q) extend to algebra maps, and the
same half-turn gives a nontrivial Clifford automorphism. Changing
the weights shows why preservation of the form is required.}

\p{A [centered Dirac operator](fdip-000R) couples a separately
chosen complex Clifford module to differences on a finite periodic
grid. Its [square and complete Fourier blocks](fdip-000S) give an
exact [zero-mode count](fdip-000T). Comparison with the
nearest-neighbor Laplacian shows that the two operators differ,
including on grids with no extra zero momenta.}

\p{The four-cycle also has an explicit
[arithmetic family](fdip-000U). Its polynomial configuration
form and half-turn extend over a localized integer ring.
[Clifford scalar extension](fdip-000V) gives the corresponding
algebra sheaf, while [distinct regularity domains](fdip-000W)
separate a nondegenerate form, the selected elimination, and
the trace ratio. Their real positive specializations recover
the finite Gaussian example.}

\p{Which data must a classification preserve for observables to survive?
The [groupoid of enriched four-cycle backgrounds](fdip-000X) retains
the reference pairing, named half-turn and retained partition. Its
stabilizers distinguish equal and unequal weights.
[Scalar descent and Gaussian transport](fdip-000Y) separate invariants
of classes from operators on changing field spaces.
[Arbitrary quadratic isometry](fdip-000Z) instead collapses positive
forms of the same dimension and can lose the pairing and projection,
even while transporting the normalized Gaussian.}

\p{Induced geometry provides one motivation. Shamir studies matter on a
dynamical random spatial lattice and obtains an effective metric under
assumptions about localized low-energy states
\citet{Abstract}{shamir1994dynamical}. That conditional construction does
not identify a four-cycle with spacetime. It makes the supplied microscopic
data, the state assumptions, and the proposed induced structure worth
distinguishing.}

\p{The larger question concerns an arithmetic scheme carrying a sheaf of
\vocabk{Clifford algebras}{ca-0006} and a finite-group action. Could a
moduli-controlled map connect such data to continuum backgrounds, and
could values of L-functions determine parameters of the resulting quantum
field theory? Recovering Standard Model or gravitational equations would
require explicit constructions and comparisons of observables. These are
research questions. The finite example below supplies incidence data,
real fields, weights, a combinatorial symmetry, and a positive Gaussian
state. Eliminating fields induces an effective quadratic form in this
model; a spacetime metric or continuum background requires further
construction.}

